<!-- ================= PROS & CONS ================= -->
<section class="section section-white" id="proscons">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">04 — The honest assessment</span>
      <h2>Pros and cons, without the brochure</h2>
      <p class="lede">Every market has a bull case and a bear case. The buyers who do well in the
      Philippines are the ones who internalise both before money moves.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="pc-grid">
      <div class="pc-card pros reveal">
        <h3><span class="pc-icon">✓</span> The case for</h3>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Unbeatable price-to-beach ratio</strong>
          <p>Direct oceanfront that would cost $3M–$8M in Florida, California or Australia is routinely
          available for $300k–$1M. You can own a real beachfront asset — not a timeshare of one — at a
          fraction of the Western price.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Genuine scarcity of beachfront</strong>
          <p>Prime shoreline in El Nido, Boracay, Mactan and Siargao is finite and largely already zoned.
          Supply-constrained locations with growing tourism have appreciated consistently, and the
          best remaining parcels are getting harder to find.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Tourism and infrastructure tailwinds</strong>
          <p>Post-pandemic international arrivals have recovered strongly; airport expansions (Cebu/MAIA,
          Palawan, Siargao), ferry networks and national infrastructure programmes continue to shorten
          distances and raise carrying capacity for tourism.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Rental income and yield</strong>
          <p>Resort towns sustain 4–8% net yields; well-run short-stay properties can outperform in the
          December–April peak. Condo units near airports and BPO hubs enjoy year-round corporate demand.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Lifestyle and quality of life</strong>
          <p>Seven thousand islands, world-class food, an English-speaking business culture, a warm
          service culture, and a deep expat and retiree ecosystem. The lifestyle case is not a footnote
          — it is half the asset.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Retirement-friendly framework</strong>
          <p>SRRV and 9G retiree visas, medical tourism, and a cost of living a fraction of Western
          countries make the Philippines one of the world's proven retiree destinations.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Deep diaspora demand</strong>
          <p>Tens of millions of overseas Filipinos are an emotionally invested, global buyer pool for
          resale — a structural demand floor that most beach markets simply do not have.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>English-language legal system</strong>
          <p>Contracts, titles, court records and professional services operate in English within a
          civil-law system with an established property-registry tradition. Diligence is straightforward
          if you pay for it properly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pc-card cons reveal">
        <h3><span class="pc-icon">✕</span> The case against</h3>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Typhoon exposure</strong>
          <p>Roughly 20 tropical cyclones enter Philippine waters every year and 9–10 make landfall.
          Haiyan (2013), Sina (2022, Palawan) and Kong-rey (2024, Siargao) show the tail risk.
          Construction must be typhoon-rated; insurance is costlier and sometimes excludes flood; and
          some locations sit in the direct typhoon belt.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Seismic and volcanic risk</strong>
          <p>The archipelago sits on the Pacific Ring of Fire. Major fault lines cross Cebu, Mindanao
          and Luzon; Bohol shook in 2013; Taal erupted in 2020. Site selection, engineering standards
          and insurance terms should reflect this — it is not a theoretical risk.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Title complexity is the norm, not the exception</strong>
          <p>Dual titles, legacy CLOA and land patents, heir-owned parcels without formal estate
          settlement, and missing annotations are common. A title that looks clean on paper can carry
          a decade of history. Professional due diligence is non-negotiable, not optional.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Foreign ownership restrictions</strong>
          <p>Foreigners cannot own land, condos are capped at 40% foreign ownership per project, and
          corporate structures are scrutinised. The rules are clear — but they constrain the structure
          of your investment and require planning.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Bureaucracy and timelines</strong>
          <p>BIR, Registry of Deeds and LGU processes are document-heavy and can be slow; transfers
          commonly take one to four months, and some offices carry backlogs. Patience and a good
          lawyer are part of the cost of doing business.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Infrastructure gaps in the "paradise" locations</strong>
          <p>Water, power, internet and road quality vary wildly. Many of the most beautiful spots
          require tanks, generators and satellite internet; travel from airports can take half a day.
          Visit in the wet season before you buy.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Liquidity is the quiet killer</strong>
          <p>Resale is a thin, slow market. Sales can take one to three years and often settle 10–20%
          below asking. If you might need the cash back, the Philippines can be a frustrating place
          to liquidate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-item">
          <strong>Flooding, seawater intrusion and tropical maintenance</strong>
          <p>Low-lying coastal lots can flood; some areas face saltwater intrusion into wells; and
          humidity, salt air and termites accelerate wear. Budget 1–3% of asset value annually for
          maintenance on a rental, and elevate and drain properly at build time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= LEGAL ================= -->
<section class="section section-dark" id="legal">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">05 — Legal essentials</span>
      <h2>Who can own what — and how to close without surprises</h2>
      <p class="lede">The Philippines is a common-sense market wrapped in strict constitutional rules.
      Understand the framework below and you will know exactly which structures are available to you,
      what a clean title looks like, and the process by which money becomes ownership.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="legal-h3 reveal">The ownership framework at a glance</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap reveal">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Owner type</th><th>Land</th><th>Condominium</th><th>Leasehold</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Philippine citizen</strong></td><td>Full ownership (subject to constitutional ceilings of ~44 ha plains / 100 ha upland for non-agricultural land)</td><td>100%</td><td>n/a</td><td>The simplest path; family and community-property rules apply</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Foreign individual</strong></td><td>✕ Not permitted directly</td><td>Up to 40% of the project's units/stock</td><td>Up to 25 years, renewable to 50 with approval; longer terms possible for qualified cases</td><td>The workhorse structures: condo, lease, or corporation</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Foreigner married to a Filipino</strong></td><td>Limited — generally may not purchase the spouse's land, but may acquire land by succession (inheritance)</td><td>Through the spouse's share or the 40% cap</td><td>Same as foreign individual</td><td>Highly fact-specific — retain independent Philippine counsel</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>60/40 corporation</strong></td><td>Through a corporation ≥60% Filipino-owned in voting stock</td><td>100%</td><td>—</td><td>Must be a genuine, operating business; sham vehicles are pierced by the SEC and BIR</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout-dark reveal">
      <h3>⚓ The foreshore rule — read this twice</h3>
      <p>The Philippine <strong>foreshore</strong> — the land between the high- and low-water marks of
      the sea and inland waters — is part of the <strong>public domain and cannot be sold or owned by
      anyone</strong>. What you buy is always the <strong>upland</strong> behind the beach, plus access.
      So on every "beachfront lot" you must verify: (1) that the title does not purport to cover
      foreshore area, (2) that you have a genuine, documented right of access to the beach (adjacent
      road, registered easement, or a fronting road), and (3) that no part of the lot sits inside a
      protected area, where private sale is prohibited. A lot with no legal beach access is not
      beachfront — it is "a field with a nice view."</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="legal-h3 reveal">Know your titles</h3>
    <p class="legal-p reveal">The document you accept from the seller determines everything. Learn the
    vocabulary before you negotiate anything:</p>
    <dl class="titles-dl reveal">
      <div class="title-item"><dt>TCT — Transfer Certificate of Title</dt><dd>The gold standard. Issued by the Registry of Deeds (ROD) after registration; it evidences registered ownership of the exact parcel described. This is what you want to see — and then verify at the ROD.</dd></div>
      <div class="title-item"><dt>CT — Certified Title</dt><dd>The pre-1990 form of registered title; equivalent in force to a TCT. Older coastal lots frequently carry CTs.</dd></div>
      <div class="title-item"><dt>Land Patent</dt><dd>A very old grant (typically pre-1955) from the government. It must be converted to a registered title before it is marketable — a process that can take years and may not succeed.</dd></div>
      <div class="title-item"><dt>CLOA / Emphyteutic lease</dt><dd>Certificate of Land Ownership Allocation (upland, generally community or agricultural land) or an emphyteutic lease. Neither is alienable like a TCT; conversion is possible but uncertain. Treat with caution, especially near the coast.</dd></div>
      <div class="title-item"><dt>Tax Declaration</dt><dd>A local-government document showing who pays real property tax. It is <strong>not a title</strong> and confers no ownership. Many frauds begin here — a beautiful beach lot "with tax declaration" and nothing more.</dd></div>
      <div class="title-item"><dt>Annotations</dt><dd>Notices entered on the title itself: mortgages, liens, easements, dations, court orders, prohibitions. Every annotation must be explained and, where possible, released before you close.</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="legal-h3 reveal">The closing process, step by step</h3>
    <ol class="timeline reveal">
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">1</span><h4>Negotiate and sign a Contract to Sell (CTS)</h4><p>Put the price, payment schedule, contingencies and closing conditions in writing. A good CTS makes due diligence a condition of your obligation to buy, and typically routes deposits through the notary or a lawyer for safekeeping until conditions are met.</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">2</span><h4>Title search at the Registry of Deeds</h4><p>Your lawyer pulls the physical title, the parent (mother) title, and the official copy. Check: registered owner, exact description and area, all annotations, and — critically — whether the same land appears in any other title (dual titling).</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">3</span><h4>Verify the seller's identity and authority</h4><p>Government IDs matched to the title; marriage certificate and <strong>spousal consent</strong> where the land is community property; for estates, the settlement of estate or order of succession with all heirs signing; for corporations, the secretary's certificate and board resolution authorising the sale.</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">4</span><h4>Litigation search</h4><p>Check the Regional Trial Court, the Muncipal Trial Courts and land cases for suits involving the parcel or the seller — boundary disputes, ejectment cases, and pending claims can surface here.</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">5</span><h4>Physical and government verification</h4><p>An independent surveyor confirms boundaries, area and markers against the title; the LGU confirms the zonal classification and that the lot is inside no protected area; BIR records confirm the Tax Declaration is current and consistent with the title.</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">6</span><h4>Negotiate taxes and fees</h4><p>Agree in the CTS who bears CGT, DST, transfer tax, registration and notarial fees. This is a genuinely negotiable point — do not let "that's just how it's done" close the conversation.</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">7</span><h4>Execute and notarise the Deed of Absolute Sale (DAS)</h4><p>The final sale document, signed by all sellers (and spouses) before a notary public. Your lawyer should review the DAS line by line — it should mirror the CTS's terms exactly.</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">8</span><h4>Pay the taxes</h4><p>CGT (6% of the higher of sale price or zonal value, or 15% of net gain if elected), Documentary Stamp Tax (0.5%), and the local transfer tax (0.5%) are computed and paid with the BIR and the LGU. Receipts become part of the registration file.</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">9</span><h4>Register the title in your name</h4><p>Your lawyer files the registration package at the Registry of Deeds. A new TCT is issued in the buyer's name — <strong>you are fully protected only when this happens</strong>, not when you pay, and not when you sign.</p></li>
      <li class="tl-step"><span class="tl-num">10</span><h4>Post-closing housekeeping</h4><p>Update the Tax Declaration, secure the property, arrange utilities, register for any rental or business permits, and file the title, deed, receipts and IDs in one safe place — physically and digitally.</p></li>
    </ol>

    <h3 class="legal-h3 reveal">Red flags — walk away, or renegotiate from strength</h3>
    <div class="redflags reveal">
      <h3>🚩 Ten warning signs of a troubled deal</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>"We only have a Tax Declaration"</strong> — no registered title, or a title that "will be fixed later." Fixing titles is a different business than buying property.</li>
        <li><strong>The seller is not the registered owner</strong> — and cannot produce the legal chain (settlement of estate, corporate authorisation, power of attorney) that explains why they can sell.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing spousal consent</strong> — land acquired during marriage is community property; one spouse cannot sell it alone, and a one-sided sale can be unwound.</li>
        <li><strong>Pressure to pay before diligence</strong> — any seller who resists a title search, a survey or a lawyer's review is telling you something.</li>
        <li><strong>A price dramatically below market</strong> — in this market, a bargain usually explains itself (a defect, a dispute, a boundary problem, a protected area).</li>
        <li><strong>No documented beach access</strong> — no fronting road, no registered easement. The lot may be landlocked or dependent on someone else's goodwill.</li>
        <li><strong>The lot overlaps a protected area</strong> — marine parks and protected landscapes (common around the best bays) cannot be privately sold or developed; check with DENR and the LGU.</li>
        <li><strong>Annotations you can't get explained</strong> — an unexplained lien or court annotation on the title is a reason to stop, not a line item to budget for.</li>
        <li><strong>"Lease with option to buy" that never converts</strong> — a well-known structure for sidestepping foreign-ownership rules; without a clear, dated, notarised conversion path, you may be a tenant, not a future owner.</li>
        <li><strong>The seller won't let you verify</strong> — IDs, marriage records, estate papers. In a clean deal, verification is routine and the seller cooperates happily.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <h3 class="legal-h3 reveal">Special situations</h3>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="card reveal" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.04);border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.12)">
        <span class="k" style="color:#E0C06B">Marriage &amp; family</span>
        <h3 style="color:#F4EFE3">Mixed-nationality couples</h3>
        <p style="color:#B9C7D4">A foreign spouse generally may not acquire the Filipino spouse's land by purchase or donation, but may inherit it — and the rules for disposition have timelines. Structure ownership with counsel <em>before</em> buying, not after, or the couple ends up with an asset it cannot cleanly sell.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card reveal" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.04);border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.12)">
        <span class="k" style="color:#E0C06B">Inheritance &amp; estate</span>
        <h3 style="color:#F4EFE3">Buying from heirs</h3>
        <p style="color:#B9C7D4">A large share of Philippine beach land is owned by families, often informally. Before paying: a formal settlement of estate or order of succession, <em>all</em> heirs signing the deed, estate tax (0.6% of the gross estate under current law) settled, and a clean title search. Paying one heir of five is how families end up in court.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card reveal" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.04);border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.12)">
        <span class="k" style="color:#E0C06B">Corporate structures</span>
        <h3 style="color:#F4EFE3">The 60/40 company</h3>
        <p style="color:#B9C7D4">Foreigners can hold land through a corporation that is at least 60% Filipino-owned in voting stock — but the company must be genuine: real officers, real business, real compliance. Shell "vehicle" companies are actively pierced by the SEC and BIR, with penalties and unwinding. Use this structure only with experienced counsel and a real operating purpose.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= FINANCE ================= -->
<section class="section section-ivory" id="finance">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">06 — Financial strategy</span>
      <h2>Money, taxes, currency and exit</h2>
      <p class="lede">The purchase is the opening move. What determines your outcome is the financing
      structure, the tax negotiation, the currency timing, the income plan, and — most often ignored —
      the exit plan.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="fin-grid">
      <div class="fin-card reveal">
        <div class="fin-k">₱</div>
        <h3>Financing: cash, credit, or in-house</h3>
        <p><strong>Cash</strong> remains the norm — and the leverage. Cash buyers routinely negotiate
        3–8% below asking, and most sellers price with cash in mind.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bank mortgages</strong> are available to Filipino citizens (roughly 7.5–12% p.a., 15–25 year terms). Foreigners are rarely bank-financed directly; some developers run in-house programmes (commonly 10–20% down, balance over 2–5 years) and a few banks lend to qualified foreign residents.</li>
          <li><strong>Offshore borrowing</strong> (your home-country bank, against the Philippine asset or portfolio) is viable for larger purchases but adds currency and structural complexity.</li>
          <li>Indicative monthly payments at ~9% p.a.:</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="table-wrap" style="margin-top:12px;margin-bottom:0">
          <table class="tbl" style="min-width:0">
            <thead><tr><th>Loan</th><th>Rate / term</th><th>Payment</th><th>Total interest</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>$250,000</td><td class="num">9% / 15 yrs</td><td class="num">≈ $2,540 / mo</td><td class="num">≈ $206k</td></tr>
              <tr><td>$500,000</td><td class="num">9% / 15 yrs</td><td class="num">≈ $5,070 / mo</td><td class="num">≈ $413k</td></tr>
              <tr><td>$1,000,000</td><td class="num">9.5% / 20 yrs</td><td class="num">≈ $9,320 / mo</td><td class="num">≈ $1.24M</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="muted small" style="margin-top:10px">Fixed-rate illustration; Philippine mortgage rates move with the central bank. Amortisation front-loads interest — price your exit before you price the payment.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="fin-card reveal">
        <div class="fin-k">%</div>
        <h3>Taxes, fees and the negotiation</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>CGT (6%)</strong> is technically the seller's tax but is customarily passed to the buyer — negotiate it. On a $1M deal it is a $60,000 conversation.</li>
          <li><strong>DST (0.5%) and transfer tax (0.5%)</strong> are the buyer's; registration is nominal. Budget 2–3% on top of CGT for these plus notary and professional fees.</li>
          <li><strong>VAT (12%)</strong> applies to VAT-registered sellers (typically developers) and is usually embedded in the price; it replaces CGT in that case.</li>
          <li><strong>Real property tax</strong> is 0.2–2.5% of the <em>assessed</em> value annually — and assessed values are often a fraction of market, so effective annual carrying cost is commonly well under 1% of value.</li>
          <li><strong>Estate tax</strong> is 0.6% of the gross estate for Philippine residents — relevant to long-hold succession planning.</li>
          <li>Put every tax allocation <strong>in the CTS</strong>. "Who pays what" is the single most negotiable line in the entire transaction.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="fin-card reveal">
        <div class="fin-k">⌂</div>
        <h3>Rental income: realistic yields</h3>
        <p>Two strategies dominate: <strong>short-stay</strong> (higher gross yield, more management,
        depends on LGU short-rental rules and seasonality) and <strong>long-stay / corporate</strong>
        (lower yield, stable, minimal management — strongest near airports and BPO hubs).</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Example: a $250k two-bedroom beachfront condo renting at ~$120/night at 55% occupancy grosses ≈ $24k/yr; after management (15–25%), utilities and turnover, net yield lands around <strong>5–6%</strong>.</li>
          <li>Peak season (December–April) can carry 70–85% occupancy; the wet season (June–November) is materially weaker. Price your cash flow on the full-year average, not the peak.</li>
          <li>Management companies typically take 15–25% plus utilities; some LGUs restrict or permit short-term rentals by ordinance — verify before buying "for Airbnb."</li>
          <li>Net yields of <strong>4–8%</strong> are the honest range for professionally managed beachfront; claims of 15%+ are usually gross, seasonal, or fiction.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="fin-card reveal">
        <div class="fin-k">⇄</div>
        <h3>Currency and timing</h3>
        <p>You are, in effect, converting USD (or your home currency) into pesos — and back again at
        exit. The peso trades in a band (roughly ₱55–58/US$ in the 2024–25 period), and that band is
        your opportunity.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Buying when the peso is at the <strong>strong</strong> end of its band materially improves your effective entry price; the reverse applies at exit.</li>
          <li><strong>Spread construction payments</strong> over the peso cycle where possible; large lump-sum payments should be timed, not just scheduled.</li>
          <li>Keep operating costs (staff, utilities, maintenance) in local peso accounts; hold your capital reserves in the currency of your home.</li>
          <li>Do not lever across currencies: a peso loan serviced from dollar income (or vice versa) creates a hidden FX position you did not intend to take.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="fin-card reveal">
        <div class="fin-k">⎋</div>
        <h3>Exit and liquidity — plan it before entry</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Resale is a <strong>thin, patient market</strong>: budget 1–3 years to sell and price 10–20% below initial asking to transact.</li>
          <li>Your buyer pool: local wealth, the diaspora (strongest for land in "home province" coastal areas), and expats (strongest for condos and turnkey villas in known towns).</li>
          <li><strong>Location is liquidity.</strong> A unit or lot within a recognised resort brand's orbit sells; a unique parcel outside it does not.</li>
          <li>Alternatives to a full sale: lease-back with the operator, long-term lease, or rental — liquidity in degrees.</li>
          <li>Keep the file immaculate (title, deeds, tax receipts, permits, photos, surveys). A documented property sells faster and at a better price than an undocumented one — every time.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="fin-card reveal">
        <div class="fin-k">🛡</div>
        <h3>Insurance and physical risk</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Carry <strong>property insurance with windstorm and typhoon coverage</strong>; scrutinise flood exclusions — they are common and expensive when surcharged.</li>
          <li>Earthquake coverage is available for built structures; verify deductibles and the insurer's claims track record in the archipelago.</li>
          <li>For new builds, contract for <strong>typhoon-rated engineering</strong> (wind, roof, glazing, drainage, elevation) — it protects the asset and is a selling point at exit.</li>
          <li>Budget <strong>1–3% of asset value per year</strong> for maintenance on a rented or unoccupied property: salt air, humidity and termites are relentless.</li>
          <li>Elevate and drain: the cheapest insurance is a well-designed foundation, a proper roof pitch, and a lot that does not flood in a 10-year storm.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tips reveal">
      <h3>✦ Six "smart money" principles</h3>
      <div class="tip-grid">
        <div class="tip"><span class="n">1</span><p><strong>Pay for diligence like it's part of the asset.</strong> A $3k–$8k due-diligence file (lawyer, title search, survey, litigation, LGU/DENR checks) has saved buyers from seven-figure mistakes. It is the highest-ROI expense in the transaction.</p></div>
        <div class="tip"><span class="n">2</span><p><strong>Buy the title, not the brochure.</strong> Every beautiful lot with a Tax Declaration and a "title coming soon" is a different, riskier asset than the one with a clean, verified TCT. Price the difference accordingly.</p></div>
        <div class="tip"><span class="n">3</span><p><strong>Negotiate the tax stack, not just the price.</strong> The CGT/transfer/registration allocation is worth more to you than a $10k discount on a $1M deal.</p></div>
        <div class="tip"><span class="n">4</span><p><strong>Visit in the wet season.</strong> One dry-season trip shows you the postcard; one typhoon-season week shows you the actual asset — water tables, flooding, road condition, power reliability.</p></div>
        <div class="tip"><span class="n">5</span><p><strong>Structure for your exit audience.</strong> Condos for expats, turnkey villas in known towns for second-home buyers, documented land in home provinces for the diaspora. Build the asset the market can buy.</p></div>
        <div class="tip"><span class="n">6</span><p><strong>Keep one Philippine professional relationship, not a transaction.</strong> A lawyer, a broker and a property manager who know you and the locality will serve you for twenty years — and will catch problems before they cost.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="calc-title reveal" style="margin-top:56px">A 12-month roadmap</h3>
    <div class="road-grid">
      <div class="road-step reveal"><span class="road-when">Months 0 – 2</span><h4>Define the thesis</h4><p>Purpose (lifestyle, yield, appreciation), budget including 10% transaction costs, target locations, and the visa plan. Shortlist three markets across two price tiers.</p></div>
      <div class="road-step reveal"><span class="road-when">Months 2 – 4</span><h4>Visit — in both seasons if you can</h4><p>One to two weeks per shortlisted market. Walk the beaches at high and low tide, talk to local brokers and builders, feel the infrastructure, and identify the micro-areas where the land is both good and titled.</p></div>
      <div class="road-step reveal"><span class="road-when">Months 4 – 6</span><h4>Assemble the team</h4><p>Engage a Philippine real-estate lawyer, an independent surveyor, and (if you're not present) a reputable broker. Review 3–5 candidate listings and run full diligence on your favourites.</p></div>
      <div class="road-step reveal"><span class="road-when">Months 6 – 9</span><h4>Negotiate and condition</h4><p>Sign the CTS with diligence, beach-access and title-contingency clauses. Agree the tax stack. Route deposits through the notary or lawyer. Do not pay for a title you have not verified.</p></div>
      <div class="road-step reveal"><span class="road-when">Months 9 – 12</span><h4>Close and register</h4><p>DAS, notarisation, tax payments, and registration of the new TCT in your name. Secure the property, set up utilities and management, and file the complete transaction record.</p></div>
      <div class="road-step reveal"><span class="road-when">Ongoing</span><h4>Operate, document, revalue</h4><p>Manage or lease the property, keep the file immaculate, track the market annually, and revisit the exit thesis every two years. The asset rewards documentation and patience.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

