<section class="section section-white" id="checklist">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal" style="text-align:center;margin:0 auto 48px">
      <span class="eyebrow" style="justify-content:center">07 — Due diligence</span>
      <h2>The pre-purchase checklist</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin:0 auto">Fourteen items, every one of which has saved a buyer from a
      costly mistake. Work through them before any money moves — tick them off as your team completes
      each verification.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="check-panel reveal">
      <div class="check-header">
        <h3>Your verification file</h3>
        <p>Progress is saved as you tick items. Nothing here is complete until a professional has confirmed it in writing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="check-progress">
        <div class="check-bar-track"><div class="check-bar" id="checkBar"></div></div>
        <div class="check-meta">
          <span class="check-count" id="checkCount">0 of 14 complete</span>
          <button class="check-reset" id="checkReset" type="button">Reset</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="check-list" id="checklist">
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Registered title verified at the Registry of Deeds</strong> — TCT/CT in hand, official copy pulled, no dual-titling against the mother title.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Seller's identity and authority confirmed</strong> — IDs match the title; spousal consent present; estate settlement or corporate authorisation in order.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>No unexplained annotations</strong> — every lien, mortgage, easement, dation or court order on the title is identified, explained and (where needed) released.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Litigation search completed</strong> — Regional Trial Court and Municipal Trial Courts checked for cases involving the parcel or the seller.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Independent survey done</strong> — boundaries, area and markers physically confirmed by your own surveyor, not the seller's.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Beach access is legal and documented</strong> — fronting road or registered easement; the foreshore is public and no part of the lot claims it.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Protected-area status cleared</strong> — DENR and LGU comprehensive land use plan confirm the lot is outside protected or reserved zones.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Tax Declaration current and consistent</strong> — assessed area and owner align with the title; arrears (if any) allocated in the CTS.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Zoning supports your intended use</strong> — residential, commercial or resort use is permitted under the local comprehensive land use plan.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Utilities, water and internet confirmed</strong> — supply sources, quality and backup (tanks, generator, satellite) understood and budgeted.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Flood, typhoon and seismic risk assessed</strong> — elevation, drainage, wind exposure and nearest fault line reviewed; insurance terms obtained in writing.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Contract to sell reviewed by your lawyer</strong> — diligence, access and title contingencies included; deposits held by notary or lawyer; tax stack agreed.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Budget covers the whole stack</strong> — purchase price, ~10% transaction costs, construction/renovation, and a 1–2 year holding reserve.</span></label>
        <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Exit strategy defined in writing</strong> — target buyer pool, realistic holding period, and price-to-sell assumptions.</span></label>
      </div>
      <p class="check-msg" id="checkMsg">Work through these before any money moves.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= FAQ ================= -->
<section class="section section-sand" id="faq">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal" style="text-align:center;margin:0 auto 48px">
      <span class="eyebrow" style="justify-content:center">08 — Questions buyers actually ask</span>
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-wrap reveal">

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can a foreigner own beachfront land in the Philippines?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p><strong>No — not directly.</strong> The 1987 Constitution reserves land ownership for Filipino
          citizens. Foreign individuals have three solid routes instead: (1) own a <strong>condominium
          unit</strong> outright, provided the foreign share of the project stays at or below 40%;
          (2) <strong>lease land</strong> for up to 25 years, renewable to a total of 50 (longer terms in
          qualified cases with government approval); or (3) hold property through a <strong>genuine
          corporation at least 60% Filipino-owned</strong> in voting stock. Each structure has
          consequences for tax, resale and succession — choose with counsel, deliberately.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What exactly does the 40% condominium cap mean?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p>Under the Condominium Act, a project's foreign ownership may not exceed <strong>40% of the
          total units/stock</strong>. In practice: the developer (and later the association) issues a
          foreign-equity certificate showing the remaining foreign-eligible units. You can buy as many
          units as you like <em>as long as the project-wide foreign share stays ≤40%</em> — but you should
          confirm the current status before closing, because a sold-out foreign quota blocks the sale.
          For citizens, there is no cap.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How long does a purchase take, end to end?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p>Typically <strong>one to four months</strong> after signing the Contract to Sell:
          title search and diligence run about 1–3 weeks; the DAS and tax payments another 2–4 weeks;
          registration at the Registry of Deeds varies by office — anywhere from a few weeks to three
          months where backlogs exist. Condo transfers from reputable developers are often faster
          (4–8 weeks) because the paperwork is standardised. Budget realistically and never treat a
          "fast office" promise as a plan.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Do I need to be in the Philippines to buy?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p><strong>No.</strong> Buyers abroad close transactions regularly using a
          <strong>Special Power of Attorney</strong> in favour of their Philippine lawyer (or a trusted
          notary), executed and authenticated at the Philippine consulate or a local notary. Your lawyer
          can sign the DAS, pay the taxes and file the registration on your behalf. That said, at least
          one site visit — including a wet-season visit — is strongly advised for land purchases.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can I get a bank loan as a foreigner?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p><strong>Rarely, directly.</strong> Philippine banks mainly mortgage Filipino citizens and
          residents. Foreigners typically buy with <strong>cash</strong> (which earns the best price —
          often 3–8% off), use <strong>developer in-house programmes</strong> (10–20% down with 2–5 year
          balances), or arrange <strong>offshore financing</strong> against their wider portfolio. Some
          banks will lend to qualified foreign residents with local income — ask, but do not build the
          plan around it.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Is a Tax Declaration enough to buy with?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p><strong>Absolutely not.</strong> A Tax Declaration shows who pays the local property tax;
          it creates no ownership right. In this market, "the title is in process" is the opening line
          of many bad deals. Accept only a registered title (TCT/CT) that has been <em>physically
          verified at the Registry of Deeds</em>, or a documented, realistic conversion path (land
          patent → title) with the timeline priced in.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What about the beach itself — who owns it?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p><strong>No one can own the foreshore.</strong> The land between the high- and low-water
          marks is public domain under Philippine law — it belongs to the state, and anyone may use the
          beach. What you own is the upland behind it. That makes <strong>access</strong> the critical
          question: a fronting road, a registered easement, or adjacency that the title and the ground
          both confirm. Verify before you fall in love with the view.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What happens if I sell — or if I die — while owning Philippine property?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p><strong>Sale</strong> is straightforward for citizens; for foreigners it depends on the
          structure (a condo is freely resellable within the 40% cap; a lease is assignable per its
          terms; a 60/40 company requires the corporate machinery). Plan the resale story at entry —
          thin markets reward the documented, conventional asset. <strong>Succession</strong>: Philippine
          estates pay 0.6% estate tax (residents); a foreign national who inherits land is generally
          expected to dispose of it within a reasonable period. A simple will and an estate plan
          prepared by a Philippine estate lawyer — alongside any will at home — keeps this from becoming
          a family project in two jurisdictions.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Are short-term (Airbnb-style) rentals allowed?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p><strong>Generally yes — with local permits.</strong> Most resort LGUs issue business or
          homestay permits for short-term rentals, and the rules vary town by town: some require
          registration, some cap nights, and a few (including parts of managed island resorts) restrict
          them entirely. Before buying "for short-stay income," obtain the current ordinance from the
          local government and confirm with a local management company what a realistic,
          permit-compliant occupancy looks like.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Which visas let me stay long-term?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">
          <p>The main routes (rules and minimums are updated periodically — confirm with the Bureau of
          Immigration):</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Tourist visa</strong> — typically 30 days on arrival, extendable in-country.</li>
            <li><strong>9A pre-approved visa</strong> — one-year multiple entry, renewable; the standard long-stay workhorse.</li>
            <li><strong>9G retiree visa</strong> — for qualifying retirees (50+) meeting income requirements.</li>
            <li><strong>SRRV (Special Resident Retiree's Visa)</strong> — the most established retiree programme, with tiered minimum deposits (commonly US$10k–$50k depending on category), tax benefits on remittances, and the right to own a condominium within the 40% cap.</li>
            <li><strong>ACR I-Card</strong> — the resident alien ID that many long-stayers obtain for banking, leasing and daily life.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>A visa is not a property requirement — you can buy before or without one — but the plan
          should be coherent: your stay structure, your ownership structure and your tax residence
          belong in one conversation with your advisers.</p>
        </div></div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= GLOSSARY ================= -->
<section class="section section-ivory" id="glossary">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">09 — The vocabulary</span>
      <h2>Glossary of terms you will meet</h2>
    </div>
    <dl class="gloss-grid reveal">
      <div class="gloss"><dt>TCT / CT</dt><dd>Transfer (or Certified) Certificate of Title — the registered title issued by the Registry of Deeds; the document of ownership you verify and ultimately receive.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>CTS</dt><dd>Contract to Sell — the preliminary sale agreement with price, schedule and conditions, signed before the final deed.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>DAS</dt><dd>Deed of Absolute Sale — the final, notarised instrument of sale that is registered to produce a new title in the buyer's name.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>ROD</dt><dd>Registry of Deeds — the government office that registers titles and records annotations; the place where you verify everything.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>BIR</dt><dd>Bureau of Internal Revenue — the national tax authority; computes CGT and DST, and issues the receipts needed for registration.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>ZVV</dt><dd>Zonal (or fair market) value — the BIR-assigned value of the area used as a floor for tax computations; taxes are based on the higher of price or ZVV.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>CGT</dt><dd>Capital Gains Tax — 6% of the higher of sale price or zonal value (or 15% of net gain if elected); the seller's tax, usually negotiated onto the buyer.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>DST</dt><dd>Documentary Stamp Tax — 0.5% of the consideration, the buyer's tax, computed on the same base as CGT.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>CLOA</dt><dd>Certificate of Land Ownership Allocation — an upland, often agricultural or community, land document; not freely alienable like a TCT and generally a diligence red flag on coastal parcels.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>Tax Declaration</dt><dd>The LGU's record of who pays real property tax. Not a title. Not ownership. Know the difference before you pay.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>PSA / SEC</dt><dd>Philippine Statistics Authority (records, incl. condominium foreign-equity certificates) and Securities and Exchange Commission (corporations, incl. the 60/40 rule).</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>LGU</dt><dd>Local Government Unit — the city or municipality; issues the Tax Declaration, permits, and the zoning rules that determine what you may build and rent.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>ACR I-Card</dt><dd>Alien Certificate of Registration Identity Card — the resident-alien ID that eases banking, leasing and daily life for long-stay foreigners.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>SRRV</dt><dd>Special Resident Retiree's Visa — the established retiree residency programme with tiered deposits and tax benefits on remittances.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>Foreshore</dt><dd>The state-owned zone between the high- and low-water marks. Public domain — unsellable, undevelopable, and the reason "beach access" is a legal question, not a scenic one.</dd></div>
      <div class="gloss"><dt>Easement</dt><dd>A registered right to cross or use another's land — how many beachfront lots legally reach the beach. Insist on seeing it, not hearing about it.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

